0040 — the SVG baker covers by scanline, and draws strokes.

`framework/compiler/bake-svg.ts` decided every 4x4 subsample of a shape's
box by testing it against every edge of the shape: pixels x 16 x edges. Real
vector art does not survive that. One path holding a hex grid of 952 cells
(11,424 edges over 1400x1000) took 23 minutes of CPU; a 300-path
illustration three; a ring at 2x one. And it drew fills only, so a HUD's rings,
grids and sparklines had to be rasterised by hand into PNGs before the
build ever saw them.

Coverage. Each sample row is crossed once per edge, with the reference's
own predicate (`ay <= py ? by > py : by <= py`) and its own arithmetic
(`ax + (bx - ax) * ((py - ay) / (by - ay))`, from the edge's first
endpoint), the crossings are sorted, and the sample columns between two
consecutive crossings share one verdict: inside when the crossings to their
right wind nonzero, or are odd under evenodd. That is the per-sample test
evaluated once per interval instead of once per sample, so every pixel's
covered count is the same integer, and the compositing after it is
untouched. A disc keeps its own test `dx * dx + dy * dy <= r * r`, which
holds on one run of columns per sample row; the run's ends are found and
the middle filled. The axis-aligned rect keeps its analytic coverage. The
same line computed from the other endpoint is NOT the same: it moves a
pixel about once in 1,500 random fills, and in 80% of the knife-edge cases
below, so the endpoint order is load-bearing and the tests hold it.

Strokes are flattened into fill contours — one quad per segment, a miter,
round or bevel wedge on the outer side of each corner (a miter past
stroke-miterlimit is a bevel), a butt, round or square cap at each end of
an open subpath, a dot or square for a zero-length one — all wound one way
and filled nonzero, so their union is the stroke and its edges get the
fill's coverage. A vertex inside a flattened curve takes a round join,
because the curve has no corner there. Arcs, circles, ellipses, rounded
corners and round joins and caps are subdivided until each chord is within
1/32 px, by bisecting unit vectors (square roots only); a span past a
quarter turn has whole quarters split off by an exact 90-degree rotation
first, so no bisection is ever of two nearly opposite vectors.

The document is walked as a tree instead of scanned for three tag names:
`<g>` composes its `transform` and passes fill and stroke properties to its
children, `opacity` on a `<g>` — and on a shape with both a fill and a
stroke — composites through a layer, `style=""` declarations count as
attributes, and `display`/`visibility` are honoured. What would paint and
is not implemented is refused rather than dropped.

Measured against the reference, `tests/fixtures/bake-svg-reference.ts`
(this file at 7dce4349, verbatim but for its `pak.ts` import, plus its two
per-sample loops lifted out unchanged):
- every SVG in this tree the reference bakes (`git ls-files '*.svg'`), at
  densities 1, 2 and 3: byte-identical — 38 files — except
  `docs/bench/three-frameworks-ppsspp-2026-07-{29,30}.svg`, whose `<text>`
  the reference dropped and this refuses; with their `<text>`, `<line>`
  and stroke attributes removed they are byte-identical too;
- 1,500 random fills (one to four contours, either rule, vertices often on
  a pixel edge or a sample position, horizontal, vertical, repeated and
  self-crossing edges, windows smaller than the shape) and 1,500 random
  discs (r = 0 and negative r included): identical counts;
- 500 knife-edge fills, whose edges pass in exact arithmetic through two
  sample positions so the verdict at those samples is decided by the last
  bit of the crossing: identical counts;
- four 24x24 windows of the hex grid: identical counts; and the whole hex
  grid, ring, illustration and sparkline below: identical sha256.

Timings, Bun 1.3.11 on an Apple-silicon Mac at load average 13 to 18 from
other work. Before: one run each, beside other runs, given as CPU time
(wall in brackets). After: the fastest of five. Every input is a generator
in `tests/fixtures/svg-bake-cases.ts`, and on each of the four rows both
bakers can draw, both produced the same sha256.

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| hex grid, one path, 11,424 edges, 1400x1000 at 1x | 1,391 s (1,766 s) | 63 ms |
| ring 1080x1080 at 2x, two bezier circles under evenodd | 60 s (80 s) | 17 ms |
| 300 cubic blobs, 1920x1080 at 1x | 178 s (275 s) | 256 ms |
| area under a 360-point sparkline, 1920x1080 at 1x | 53 s (72 s) | 15 ms |
| the hex grid as one stroked path (952 hexagons, width 2) | not drawable | 54 ms |
| the ring as `<circle fill="none" stroke-width="20">` at 2x | not drawable | 24 ms |
| the sparkline as a stroked `<polyline>`, round joins and caps | not drawable | 6 ms |

What changes besides speed, each checked against the reference:
- refused where it was dropped or misread: `<text>`, `<image>`, `<use>`,
  `<style>`, a nested `<svg>`, `<a>`, `<switch>`, `<foreignObject>` and any
  element not listed; `clip-path`, `mask`, `filter`, `stroke-dasharray`,
  `stroke-dashoffset`, markers, `paint-order`, `vector-effect` and
  `mix-blend-mode` at anything but their no-op value; `preserveAspectRatio`
  other than `none`; `transform` on the root; a transform function other
  than the six (silently skipped before); a path command letter outside
  `MLHVCSQTAZ` or any other stray character (skipped before); path data
  not starting with a moveto; a number after Z (an endless loop before);
- drawn where nothing, or the wrong thing, was: `transform` on a circle or
  rect (ignored before); `rx`/`ry` (square corners before); `fill="none"`
  on a circle or rect (refused before); paint on a `<g>` (ignored); a
  transform list, which was applied leftmost first — the reverse of SVG,
  so `translate(8 0) scale(2)` landed at 16..24 instead of 8..16; `A`
  arcs (the letter was skipped and its numbers read as line-tos);
  exponents written `E`; a circle with no cx or cy (refused as missing
  before; 0, as SVG says); a drawing command straight after Z, which now
  starts at the subpath's start point;
- not drawn where it was: shapes inside a comment, and shapes inside a
  `<clipPath>`, `<mask>` or gradient outside `<defs>`;
- kept as they were, because the shipped icons are drawn against them: a
  `<path>` (and now `<polyline>`, `<polygon>`) with no `fill` is not
  filled; the viewBox is stretched onto width x height per axis; a
  circle's radius takes the smaller of the two scales; a cubic or
  quadratic segment is 16 steps (off a true circle by 0.32 px at 250 px
  radius, 1.27 px at 1000 px — a large curve belongs in `A` or a shape;
  flattened adaptively to 1/32 px instead, 1 shipped icon moves at 1x, 10
  at 2x and 11 at 3x);
  `rotate()` goes through `Math.cos`/`Math.sin`, as do `skewX`/`skewY`
  (`Math.tan`) and an arc's x-axis-rotation.

By file:
- `framework/compiler/bake-svg.ts`: the baker. `coverPath` and
  `coverCircle` are exported for the tests.
- `tests/fixtures/bake-svg-reference.ts`: the reference, as above.
- `tests/fixtures/svg-bake-cases.ts`: the seeded generators — random and
  knife-edge fills, random discs, the hex grid, ring, illustration and
  sparkline.
- `tests/svg-bake.test.ts`: the identity checks above, the hex grid and
  its stroked twin inside 5 s, and the strokes, joins, caps, arcs,
  transforms, groups and refusals. Each of these edits turns it red:
  the crossing from the other endpoint (random and knife-edge fills); `<`
  made `<=` at a sample boundary (random and knife-edge fills, and four
  shipped icons);
  the disc's left candidate column dropped (random discs); the transform
  list applied leftmost first; every miter made a bevel; fill and stroke
  composited separately under `opacity`; a closed two-point subpath given
  caps.

